.TH HSH 1 "September 2026" "1.0" "Simple Shell Manual"
.SH NAME
hsh \- simple UNIX command interpreter
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ./hsh
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hsh
is a simple UNIX command language interpreter that reads commands from
standard input, from a pipe, or from a file, and executes them.
.PP
For every line read,
.B hsh
splits the line into a command and its arguments, locates the program,
creates a child process with
.BR fork (2),
replaces that child with the requested program using
.BR execve (2),
and waits for it to terminate before displaying the prompt again.
.PP
If the command name contains a
.B /
it is used as a path directly. Otherwise
.B hsh
searches each directory listed in the
.B PATH
environment variable, in order, and runs the first executable match.
If no match is found, no child process is created.
.PP
The command lines are simple: no semicolons, no pipes, no redirections,
no quoting, no globbing and no variable expansion.
.SH INVOCATION
.B hsh
runs in two modes.
.TP
.B Interactive
When standard input is a terminal,
.B hsh
displays the prompt
.B "#cisfun$ "
and waits for the user to type a command. The prompt is displayed again
after each command completes.
.IP
.B ./hsh
.TP
.B Non\-interactive
When standard input is a pipe or a file, no prompt is printed. Each line
is read and executed until end\-of\-file is reached.
.IP
.B echo "/bin/ls" | ./hsh
.IP
.B cat commands.txt | ./hsh
.SH BUILTINS
The following commands are handled by the shell itself, without creating
a new process.
.TP
.B exit
Exits the shell, returning the exit status of the last command executed.
Arguments to
.B exit
are ignored.
.TP
.B env
Prints the current environment, one
.I NAME=value
pair per line.
.SH "END OF FILE"
Typing
.B Ctrl+D
on an empty line, or reaching the end of the input stream, terminates the
shell. In interactive mode a newline is printed before exiting.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PATH
A colon\-separated list of directories searched for executable programs.
If
.B PATH
is unset or empty, only commands given with an explicit path can be run.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.B hsh
returns the exit status of the last command executed.
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 127
The command could not be found.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run a command with arguments:
.IP
.nf
#cisfun$ ls -l /tmp
.fi
.PP
Print the environment:
.IP
.nf
#cisfun$ env
.fi
.PP
A command that does not exist:
.IP
.nf
$ echo "qwerty" | ./hsh
\&./hsh: 1: qwerty: not found
.fi
.PP
The name printed in the error message is always
.BR argv[0] ,
so invoking the shell by another path changes it:
.IP
.nf
$ echo "qwerty" | ./././hsh
\&./././hsh: 1: qwerty: not found
.fi
.SH BUGS
No known bugs. Advanced shell features such as pipes, redirections,
command separators, quoting, aliases and job control are intentionally
not implemented.
